  "performance_estimation": {
    "assumptions": [
      "Use the supplied synthesis binding for archive SHA-256 72e9a1122787e367629e67ba49864f057d6cd9dce14c7c5b86febbc5fc36951d; no older report or board-integration testbench RTL is used.",
      "The configured workload is 16 sequence positions for batch size 1. The generated block framing is 768 hidden elements over 4 lanes, or 192 input beats per token; each ingress beat is 4 x 32 bits.",
      "Initial layer weights are preloaded. The declared alternating weight buffers and next-layer prefetch are assumed to complete by each layer boundary; memory-transfer stalls are excluded and exposed handoff is zero.",
      "Nominal no-stall arithmetic uses serialized issue/wait IP service bounded by the configured C_Latency and RTL state transitions, with result-ready high. No independent streaming II is assumed for those calls.",
      "The final-token value is measured from the actual start of token 15. The source has a one-time short token-0-to-token-1 input-start interval; later input starts have the reported steady-state interval. The framework's constant-II aggregation cannot encode that exceptional first interval."
    ],
    "counter_source": "source_schedule_analysis",
    "derivation": "GeneratedAcceleratorTop.sv instantiates one GPT2PreLNBlock.sv; this is one physical Transformer block reused sequentially for 12 layers. runtime_config.json and GeneratedDesignParams.scala give the dimensions and declare double-buffered layer-weight prefetch. GeneratedAxiDdrTop.sv permits the first input transfer on the accepted start edge, so start_to_first_input_cycles=0. The actual ingress token framing is 192 beats (768/4), not one StreamBeat.\n\nSerialized FP IP service uses the matching RTL wrappers and Tcl/manifest configuration: multiply=9, add/subtract=12, compare=3, divide/sqrt=29, and integer conversion=7 cycles of configured latency. The Linear nested FSM additionally spends one cycle in row-read, one in multiply issue, one in accumulator-add issue, and waits for the serialized results: 1+1+9+1+12=24 cycles per MAC group.\n\nPer-stage service/fill/drain schedule (cycles):\n- Norm1: collects 192 source beats; its 768-lane statistics loop and scalar normalization are sequence barriers. It then emits 192 beats with a 49-cycle beat service (read/capture, center, normalize, scale, beta, transfer), giving 191*49=9,359 cycles between first and last normalized beats. This prepares the next token while QKV computes.\n- QKV Linear: 576 output beats; each costs 2 bias-read/capture cycles + 192*4*24 MAC-group cycles + 1 transfer = 18,435. Full compute service Cq=576*18,435=10,618,560. The following normalized-vector beat span and ready edge make the steady source-token interval Cq+9,359+1=10,627,920.\n- QKV formatter: 12 heads*8 beats/head=96 QKV beats; each takes 3 fields*8 lanes*2 read/capture cycles + 1 output cycle =49, or 4,704 cycles. It overlaps the next QKV Linear computation and does not set source II.\n- Attention and output projection: Attention accepts QKV into its memories while processing rows sequentially. For final causal row 15, each head takes 16*(2 read/capture + 64*23 score MAC cycles + 11 scale/write cycles)=23,760 score cycles, followed by context initialization, 48 feed cycles, Softmax max/exp/probability work (92+576+656=1,324), and 31 final context-accumulate/finalize cycles: 25,164 cycles/head, or 301,968 for 12 heads. The final head-to-projection path is 1 wait + 12*(read/capture/feed)=37 cycles. Linear_1 then computes 192 outputs at 18,435 cycles each: 3,539,520 cycles. The connected path therefore includes the final attention output projection, not merely the attention context.\n- ResidualAdd1 and Norm2: ResidualAdd1 joins the computed and saved-residual streams; its parallel four-lane add and FIFO service are hidden by the 18,435-cycle attention-output beat spacing. Norm2 has the same 20,465-cycle accumulation/scalar work after its final input beat and 192*49=9,408 cycles of emit service. The FIFO/add path adds 19 cycles before the last Norm2 input transfer; Norm2's last output beat reaches FFN input 29,873 cycles after that input.\n- FFN up/activation: Linear_2 has 192 input beats and 768 output beats. Its per-output service is 2+192*4*24+1=18,435; full output service Cup=14,158,080. Activation is the generated combinational GELU-new mapping; the depth-2 FIFO is the relevant join/backpressure buffer.\n- FFN down and final residual: Linear_3 has 768 input beats and 192 output beats, with per-output service 2+768*4*24+1=73,731 and full service D=14,156,352. During D, the depth-2 up/down FIFO holds two beats and the up Linear can hold its next computed beat at emit. Consequently consecutive completed up vectors are separated by D+5+765*18,435=28,259,132 cycles (FIFO read/ready edges account for the 5 cycles); this is slower than the source II and governs drain. ResidualAdd2's four parallel adders and output FIFO add 19 cycles after the final down-output transfer and do not throttle the 73,731-cycle output spacing.\n\nThe first up-vector completion is 28,408,350 cycles after token 0's first source beat: Norm1 first normalized beat at 20,705, QKV fill/compute/format, row-0 attention plus projection, residual/Norm2, then FFN-up compute. With 15 subsequent FFN-vector intervals and final down/residual drain, final block output is at 28,408,350+15*28,259,132+14,156,352+22=466,451,704 cycles from token 0 start. The actual token-1 first beat is at 30,065 cycles; subsequent source starts are 10,627,920 cycles apart, so token 15 starts at 30,065+14*10,627,920=148,820,945. Thus last_token_start_to_completion_cycles=466,451,704-148,820,945=317,630,759. The framework's repeated-II expression would charge 15 steady intervals rather than the actual 14 steady intervals plus the 30,065-cycle warm-up interval; it therefore overcounts this source schedule by 10,597,855 cycles per block pass if used literally. This is a representation caveat, not an unmeasured hardware result.",
    "estimate_scope": "generated_pipeline_schedule",
